om\nHurricane Polo, are expected to produce numerous showers and\nembedded thunderstorms. Heavy rain falling on saturated soils and\nburn scars could lead to localized flash flooding and rises on\nlocal rivers.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.bd99f2cf2c456b0afdad64dc05567baddbab62ce.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.bd99f2cf2c456b0afdad64dc05567baddbab62ce.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 25 at 2:21PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nIn the wake of today's precipitation, our attention will turn to the\nEastern Pacific. Tropical moisture is anticipated to spread across\nthe Desert Southwest early next week, drawing moisture from a pair of\nHurricanes; Odalys and Polo. The forecast becomes increasing complex\ndue to a closed low currently forecast to drive across the Southwest\nCONUS as Hurricane Polo approaches the Baja California Peninsula.\nStrong southerly winds ahead of the low are expected to pull tropical\nmoisture towards eastern Utah and western Colorado as early as Monday\nmorning. The challenge and uncertainty in our precipitation forecast\nincrease as the week progresses and the low traverses our southern\nStates. Excessive rainfall is most probable over AZ and NM, attm, but\nthe northerly extent poses a threat to our southern San Juan river\nbasins. Current forecasts suggests 1 to 3 inches of rain over Monday\nmorning through Wednesday. At face value, rivers and tributaries will\nsee a bump up due to said moisture. However, localized heavier\namounts could result in minor flooding across the southern San Juan\nfoothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been observed over the\nprevious 7 days. However, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts\n(i.e. above 90th percentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action\nstage to minor flooding. Most probable basins to see significant\nrises include; the Rio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito\nCreek, and the Animas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Please continue to monitor the forecast as these systems\nevolve. Especially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in\nthe southwest quadrant of the forecast area.","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-29T05:44:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 290544 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 1144 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Scattered to widespread precipitation showers and storms continue. - Increased flooding concerns for rain-soaked terrain, especially within and adjacent to the southern San Juans. - A Flood Watch is in effect for southeast Utah and southwest Colorado thro","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-28T19:09:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION...Deep moisture will continue to stream in from the southwest bringing widespread rain and a few embedded thunderstorms over the next few days. A Flood Watch has been issued for southeast UT and southwest CO due to the increased rainfall and flooding risks. All the clouds and precipitation will keep fire weather concerns to a minimum. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHO","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"rgw01","station_name":"Ridgway","distance_km":16.8,"soil_temp_5cm_f":54.45,"soil_temp_15cm_f":56.53,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":0.03,"wind_speed_mph":3.47,"air_temp_f":49.32,"relative_humidity":0.911,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu/station/rgw01"},"aviation":{"icao":"KMTJ","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KMTJ 290553Z AUTO 15005KT 10SM OVC075 13/12 A3001 RMK AO2 SLP108 T01280122 10139 20128 51008","temp_f":55,"wind_dir":150,"wind_kt":5,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"OVC","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1016.3,"observed":1790661180,"raw_taf":"TAF KMTJ 290537Z 2906/3006 10003KT P6SM VCSH BKN060 OVC070 PROB30 2906/2912 -SHRA SCT030 OVC050 FM291200 16005KT P6SM VCSH BKN020 OVC045 FM291800 34005KT P6SM VCSH SCT040 BKN110 PROB30 2919/2924 -SHRA BKN050 FM300000 VRB04KT P6SM VCSH SCT040 BKN110 PROB30 3000/3006 -SHRA SCT015 OVC035","url":"https://aviationweather.gov/data/metar/?ids=KMTJ&hours=0"},"airgradient":null,"airnow":{"aqi":4,"category":"Good","parameter":"PM2.5","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-28 23","by_parameter":{"PM2.5":{"aqi":4,"category":"Good","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-28 23"},"PM10":{"aqi":3,"category":"Good","reporting_area":"Tellurid